lesson.body:
Object-oriented programming gives you more than one way to relate classes to each other. One relationship, covered elsewhere, is inheritance: a class that is a more specific version of another class, a SportsCar is a Car. Composition is a different, equally important relationship, and it's worth keeping the two clearly separate in your mind. Composition means one class has an instance of another class stored as one of its own attributes, built and connected through the ordinary objects and attributes you already know: a Car doesn't have to be a kind of Engine, it simply has an Engine, as one piece of what makes it up.

This "has-a" relationship is powerful because it lets you build a complicated object out of smaller objects that each do one job well and can be understood, tested, and reused on their own. The bigger object doesn't need to reimplement what the smaller object already does, it just holds onto it and calls its methods when needed, a pattern often called delegation.

```python
class Engine:
    def __init__(self, horsepower):
        self.horsepower = horsepower

    def start(self):
        return f"Engine roars to life with {self.horsepower} HP."

class Car:
    def __init__(self, make, horsepower):
        self.make = make
        self.engine = Engine(horsepower)

    def start(self):
        return f"{self.make}: {self.engine.start()}"

my_car = Car("Volt", 150)
print(my_car.start())
# Output: Volt: Engine roars to life with 150 HP.
```

Trace through the construction first. Car("Volt", 150) runs Car's __init__, which sets self.make to "Volt" and then, notably, builds a whole separate object, Engine(150), and stores it in self.engine. That's composition in action: my_car doesn't inherit anything from Engine and isn't a kind of Engine, it simply owns one, reachable through self.engine exactly the way any other attribute would be. When my_car.start() is called, it doesn't recompute anything about horsepower itself, it reaches into self.engine and calls self.engine.start(), which runs Engine's own start method and returns "Engine roars to life with 150 HP." Car's start method then wraps that result in an f-string with its own make, producing "Volt: Engine roars to life with 150 HP.", which is what gets printed.

Notice how cleanly the responsibilities split: Engine knows everything about starting an engine and nothing about cars, Car knows about makes and delegates the engine-specific work to the Engine object it holds. If you needed an ElectricEngine with a totally different start method, you could swap it into Car's __init__ without touching Engine at all, something that's much harder to do cleanly with inheritance's tighter is-a relationship. That flexibility, assembling behavior out of independent, swappable parts rather than locking a class into a rigid family hierarchy, is exactly why composition is often the first tool experienced object-oriented programmers reach for.
